WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.160
- I've shared some pretty
wild stories with you guys

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:05.150
through the years on this YouTube channel,

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:07.667
but I thought we'd watch
a very popular show called

00:00:07.667 --> 00:00:09.470
"Untold Stories of the ER".

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:12.010
But before we get to
it, I have to urge you.

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:15.510
Click that notification bell
and set up all notifications.

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:17.430
That way you're sure to get my videos.

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:18.375
Let's get started.

00:00:18.375 --> 00:00:19.208
Bee-woop!

00:00:19.208 --> 00:00:20.403
- These are the real stories of the ER

00:00:20.403 --> 00:00:22.374
that doctors never talk about.

00:00:22.374 --> 00:00:23.700
(laughs)
- Already!

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:25.710
I'm gonna call BS on that one

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:28.720
because ER doctors love
sharing their stories.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.540
This isn't even about ER doctors.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:32.440
Doctors love sharing their stories.

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:34.330
And I know many people will jump and say,

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:35.163
what about HIPAA?

00:00:35.163 --> 00:00:36.700
What about patient privacy?

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:39.400
If we don't give the patients identifiers,

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.730
their name, their date of
birth, a picture of them,

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:43.930
it's totally okay to
talk about their case.

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:47.100
Otherwise, how in the world
would doctors ever learn?

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:48.552
We have to talk about cases.

00:00:48.552 --> 00:00:50.052
- Are you alright?

00:00:51.919 --> 00:00:52.752
(screams)

00:00:52.752 --> 00:00:54.930
- It's Scott Reed, I'm a paramedic.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:56.930
I said, send the ambulance routine.

00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:58.850
We don't need lights and sirens.

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:00.980
We don't need the fire department.

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:02.210
Just send an ambulance routine,

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:04.100
I think she just fainted
on the bathroom floor.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:05.720
- If this ever happens to you,

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:08.160
call 9-1-1 and tell
'em to bring everybody.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.980
In this situation, it's quite specific

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:12.000
'cause he's a paramedic,
he knows what's going on.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.420
I prefer that right
away you call for help.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:16.640
If a person's unconscious
and you aren't sure,

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:18.000
start chest compressions.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.250
That could be a difference
between saving their life

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:21.680
and them losing their life

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:23.970
just because you were self-conscious

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:25.800
or weren't sure if you
should do chest compressions.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:27.850
If you aren't sure, the
person's unconscious,

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:28.870
you've called for help,

00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:32.320
chest compressions, chest
compressions, chest compressions.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:34.680
- [Operator] We're on route
with a 38-year-old female

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:35.650
who's had a syncopal episode.

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:36.770
She's semi-conscious.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:39.100
Blood pressure is 60 over 40 and dropping.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:41.350
- Okay, so that's the most
dangerous part about all of this.

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:43.190
The fact that her blood
pressure's dropping

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:44.960
means that she's not
getting enough circulation

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:45.930
to her vital organs.

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:48.130
Think brain, kidneys, liver.

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:49.310
And when that happens,

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:51.830
the only option for us,
in the medical community,

00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:54.480
is to give a ton of fluids to
increase the blood pressure

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.640
into the circulatory system.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.170
If that doesn't work,

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:01.200
our next resort is to go
to artificial pressers

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.590
which are basically
adrenaline-like medications

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:06.060
that cause all your blood
vessels to constrict really hard

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:07.350
to raise the blood pressure

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:09.580
to make sure that the blood
reaches everywhere in your body

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:10.760
that it's supposed to.

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:13.020
If that doesn't happen in time,

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:14.820
that could be a cause of
death, it's called shock.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:16.730
So basically she's going into
shock and we don't know why.

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:19.440
- An ECG, a stat, portable chest x-ray.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:20.924
CBC, E7,

00:02:20.924 --> 00:02:24.160
(nervous exhale)
Troponin, a D-Dimer, an ABG.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:26.220
The stat did say cardiology consult.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:27.640
I need an Echo right away please.

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:30.120
- Okay so all these tests that
he's ordering all make sense,

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.350
these are all the tests
that you'd want to know

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:35.000
just to possibly get an
idea of what's going on.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:36.820
When a patient comes into the ER,

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:37.653
you don't know anything.

00:02:37.653 --> 00:02:39.200
You don't know if they're anemic,

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:40.690
you don't know if they have an infection,

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:42.880
you don't know if they
have an pneumothorax.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:44.250
Has their lung collapsed?

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:45.610
Is she having a heart attack?

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:48.930
So you need all of these tests
done as quickly as possible

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:51.290
so you can slowly start
stabilizing the patient

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:52.560
and creating a course of action.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:53.953
- She's apneic, we're gonna lose her.

00:02:53.953 --> 00:02:55.530
Bag her, bag her.

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:57.640
- So when a person loses
consciousness like that

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:58.940
and they're out of it

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:00.490
and you have to breathe for them,

00:03:00.490 --> 00:03:02.010
right away that's a sign that

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:03.680
that patient may need to be intubated.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:06.010
Intubate is when you put a
breathing tube down for them.

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:07.740
Obviously, the most important part of that

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:08.940
is to help them breathe.

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:10.460
But the second most important part

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:12.100
is to protect their airway

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:13.620
because if she were to vomit,

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:16.260
then that vomit can go
right into her lungs

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:18.080
and cause even a more serious problem.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:19.370
When a patient is unconscious,

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:22.060
they don't have a gag
reflex to protect them

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:24.050
from inhaling vomit.

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:26.890
So that's why we rush
right away to intubate.

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:28.860
- Yet, for Dr. Christopher Michos.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:30.690
- This Dr. Michos needs to tell me

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:32.730
what's going on with his scrubs.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:34.320
I like cool scrubs,

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.000
but I don't know what's
happening with those.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.300
Maybe this episode is from
a really long time ago

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:39.520
and those were in then.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:41.510
I think I'm gonna have to make him like,

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:44.700
send him a sample of
my upcoming scrub line.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:48.050
- His face and whole
body was covered in hives

00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:50.080
and he was retracting.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:52.840
His muscles were being
pulled into his chest.

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:54.910
This is a critically sick child.

00:03:54.910 --> 00:03:57.750
The serious medical condition
known as anaphylaxis.

00:03:57.750 --> 00:04:00.050
- So two things happen
during anaphylactic shock

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:01.240
that are very dangerous.

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:04.230
One is that the oral
airway starts swelling

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:05.770
and when that happens, obviously,

00:04:05.770 --> 00:04:07.970
you can lose your ability to breathe.

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:10.650
The second thing that happens
is because you have shock,

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:12.550
all of your blood vessels dilate,

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:14.570
and therefore it drops
your blood pressure.

00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:16.880
This is another form of shock
that can be quite deadly

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:19.000
'cause again you're not
perfusing and getting blood flow

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:20.720
to all your vital organs.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:22.190
Anaphylaxis is deadly.

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:24.650
That's why we hand out
epi-pens to all of our patients

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:26.480
who have gone into anaphylaxis

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:29.410
or are at risk of going into anaphylaxis.

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:30.900
That's why when epi-pens are expensive

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:31.750
and people can't afford them,

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:32.630
I get really mad.

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:34.880
- We gave him additional antihistamine.

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:37.100
We gave him intravenous steroids.

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:40.800
- So antihistamines are great
for an allergic reaction

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.020
but they don't do anything
to treat the symptoms

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:44.060
of anaphylactic shock.

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:45.250
What you really want to do

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:48.230
is make sure that the
patient has a secured airway

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:50.500
and that they're getting enough blood flow

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:51.560
to all their vital organs

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:54.630
which can mean giving them
a ton of fluids or pressers.

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:58.180
- It was about 3:00 AM on a Saturday night

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:00.130
and the bars had been let out,

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:03.550
most of the trauma that you're
gonna see has come and gone

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:06.030
and it gets quieter in the ER.

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:07.030
- That's actually true.

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:10.700
When you're on-call, you
do have this eerie silence

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:13.970
that starts setting in
around 3:30, 4:00 AM

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:17.050
and that's usually the time
where before our morning rounds,

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:18.430
if you're on a 24-hour shift,

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:20.730
maybe you can sneak in a one-hour nap.

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:24.110
I will say that after that
nap, you tend to feel worse

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:25.870
'cause you wake up and you're just jarred

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:27.610
and you want to continue sleeping.

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:31.430
So my general advice for
medical students, residents

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:33.410
or anyone who's doing
those types of shifts,

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:34.243
keep it short.

00:05:34.243 --> 00:05:37.010
Do a 30-minute nap and then
get up and do some work

00:05:37.010 --> 00:05:38.810
because if you start
entering the REM cycle

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:39.930
and come out too early,

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:41.180
that could be really problematic

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:42.620
and you'll wake up all groggy.

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:45.210
- [Nurse] 18 years old,
missing left forearm.

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:46.290
Didn't do vitals yet.

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:47.610
- [Doctor] Okay, Becky get vitals.

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:48.500
Let's get him on the bed.

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:49.730
- Did any of those cuts,

00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:50.810
are they on the abdomen?

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:53.210
Did any of them go into lungs?

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:54.230
Into the stomach?

00:05:54.230 --> 00:05:56.380
Anywhere that we need
to deal with right now?

00:05:56.380 --> 00:05:59.090
- The first thing we do in
these types of trauma situations

00:05:59.090 --> 00:06:01.830
is undress the patient
as quickly as possible

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:04.160
'cause it's very easy to miss a wound

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:06.490
if you don't have the patient
there completely naked.

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:08.760
And that sounds like a weird thing to do

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:13.760
but if you step one foot into
any level one trauma center,

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:16.590
you will see the first thing doctors do,

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:18.600
take scissors and cut off all the clothes.

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:20.640
We need to see if they're
bleeding from anywhere

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:22.640
because if they have a
puncture wound in their abdomen

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:23.473
and we miss it,

00:06:23.473 --> 00:06:25.220
and they're pooling
blood into their abdomen,

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:27.340
they can just go ahead and
die right in that moment

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:29.120
without us being able to even help them.

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:30.253
- Let's open in chest.

00:06:31.495 --> 00:06:32.370
- You wanna do that here?

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:34.730
- [Narrator] The procedure
is called a thoracotomy

00:06:34.730 --> 00:06:36.270
and it's almost never performed

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:38.850
outside the sterile environment
of an operating room.

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:40.610
- I've never heard of a thoracotomy

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:42.640
being performed in an ER, ever.

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:43.473
- Scalpel.

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:46.950
- At least try and create a sterile field.

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:49.130
Don't just say we're gonna
open up her chest, scalpel.

00:06:49.130 --> 00:06:51.310
Angie doesn't currently have a pulse.

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:55.040
I feel like a lot is inaccurate
in this representation.

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:55.873
- What did he say?

00:06:55.873 --> 00:06:56.706
- I don't know.

00:06:56.706 --> 00:06:58.447
You're gonna have to talk to the mother.

00:06:58.447 --> 00:06:59.623
- You speak Greek.

00:07:00.723 --> 00:07:01.700
- You're Greek?

00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:02.850
You speak Greek?

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:04.370
- My wife is fluent in Greek

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:05.860
and she would be happy to help.

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:08.550
I'm in a bit of a situation
here and I need your help.

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:09.740
Can you help me translate?

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:11.020
- So this is kind of messed up

00:07:11.020 --> 00:07:12.930
because it is a HIPAA violation,

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:14.580
it's like a patient privacy issue.

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:15.940
But in an emergent situation,

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:17.460
perhaps that could be put on hold.

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:18.970
What happens in regular hospitals,

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:20.600
we have translator phones for this

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:22.520
where one person picks up one phone

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:24.060
and the other picks up the other

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:26.220
and through a translator or interpreter,

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:28.120
we can actually have
a normal conversation.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:29.780
I don't know why he needs to call his wife

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:30.613
and get her involved.

00:07:30.613 --> 00:07:32.380
- Sometimes it's like
a needle in a haystack

00:07:32.380 --> 00:07:35.090
trying to figure out what's
causing the allergic reaction.

00:07:35.090 --> 00:07:38.070
- If a child is having a
severe allergic reaction,

00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:39.320
an anaphylactic shock,

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:41.400
it's not of utmost important in the ER

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:43.200
to figure out what it was from.

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:45.530
The most important thing
is to stabilize the patient

00:07:45.530 --> 00:07:48.360
using epinephrine and if that fails,

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:50.870
to put a breathing tube
in and then give steroids,

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:52.170
high doses of steroids,

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:55.190
keep the pressure up through
fluids and or pressers.

00:07:55.190 --> 00:07:57.370
And then later down the line
we can start figuring this out.

00:07:57.370 --> 00:08:00.220
I'm surprised he started doing
all this investigation here.

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:03.100
- Low and behold, I'm looking at the apron

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:04.760
and I'm thinking food.

00:08:04.760 --> 00:08:06.360
And not because I was hungry.

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:08.610
Papu, your apron.

00:08:08.610 --> 00:08:09.866
Nuts, nuts.

00:08:09.866 --> 00:08:11.140
(laughs)

00:08:11.140 --> 00:08:13.100
Whenever a doctor is yelling nuts at you,

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:14.270
you're doing something wrong.

00:08:14.270 --> 00:08:15.760
- Stay away from those nuts.

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:18.760
He would have to carry
an epi-pen to counteract

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:20.910
should he ever come in
contact with nuts again.

00:08:20.910 --> 00:08:22.580
- Please carry your epi-pens

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:24.350
and make sure they're not expired.

00:08:24.350 --> 00:08:26.040
Don't want to be over the Atlantic Ocean

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:29.050
having me stab you with a giant epi-pen

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:31.270
that's not made to be used as an epi-pen,

00:08:31.270 --> 00:08:34.440
it's just epinephrine for
someone's heart stopping.

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:36.170
- [Narrator] Nurse Penny Reed has survived

00:08:36.170 --> 00:08:38.350
an eight-hour open-heart surgery.

00:08:38.350 --> 00:08:40.020
- Feeling that my mom might not be able

00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:43.750
to be a normal person again
after all of that was scary.

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:45.760
- That's the thing, when
someone loses their pulse

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:46.650
for that long,

00:08:46.650 --> 00:08:49.490
patients' families have
questions for us like

00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:50.780
how is this gonna affect them?

00:08:50.780 --> 00:08:51.810
Are they gonna be the same?

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:55.810
And many times, we just
don't have an accurate answer

00:08:55.810 --> 00:08:57.940
so the most honest thing we can say is

00:08:57.940 --> 00:09:00.190
we don't know, but
we'll hope for the best.

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:01.830
Sometimes people don't like that,

00:09:01.830 --> 00:09:04.110
but unfortunately
medicine is as much an art

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:05.310
as it a science.

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:07.860
- When I woke up, they told
me I'd had open-heart surgery

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:10.610
for blood clots and they told
me I'd had a thoracotomy.

00:09:10.610 --> 00:09:12.930
- You don't always get a lot of victories.

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:16.390
So it was the best moment
of my career so far.

00:09:16.390 --> 00:09:19.030
- That's one of the great
things of being an ER doctor.

00:09:19.030 --> 00:09:21.190
When you intervene in a patient's case,

00:09:21.190 --> 00:09:23.810
you get the result and that
gratification right away.

00:09:23.810 --> 00:09:25.560
As a family medicine doctor,

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:29.090
a lot of times I'll give
a treatment or a plan

00:09:29.090 --> 00:09:32.210
that will take months or
years to even come to fruition

00:09:32.210 --> 00:09:33.600
when we're talking about weight loss,

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:35.100
reducing cancer risk,

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:38.410
it's not as instantly
gratifying as a surgeon

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:40.140
or as an ER doctor

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:42.970
but moments like this are
really, really powerful

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:44.640
and hard to come by as doctors.

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:46.710
- [Narrator] Jason Exner
still works in the same

00:09:46.710 --> 00:09:48.550
small community hospital

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:50.100
along with a new colleague.

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:53.400
- Haha, I can't imagine how
many puns they share together.

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:56.900
Hey, if you don't get me my tools,

00:09:56.900 --> 00:09:58.960
I'm gonna take your heart back.

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:00.450
Okay that was corny, sorry.

00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:03.210
The YouTube algorithm has
been a little bit funky lately

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:06.760
so in case you missed my
last three amazing videos,

00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:08.220
click here for the playlist

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:10.529
and as always, stay happy and healthy.

00:10:10.529 --> 00:10:13.112
(upbeat music)

